{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful noble who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He held this position for many years while serving as a key figure in the Latin East. His life was defined by political maneuvering and military conflicts against Muslim forces. Raymond faced numerous challenges including imprisonment by Nur ad-Din and later struggles with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His story reflects the complex nature of Crusader politics where alliances shifted constantly.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond was born into a prominent family and became Count of Tripoli after his father died. He was still a child when he assumed power so regents governed on his behalf until he reached adulthood. This period allowed him to learn the arts of war and diplomacy from experienced leaders. He eventually took full control of his territories and expanded his influence throughout the region. His rule brought stability to Tripoli during a turbulent era of constant warfare.\n\n*Imprisonment and Political Struggles*\nNur ad-Din captured Raymond during a military campaign and held him in captivity for many years. This event created a power vacuum that various factions tried to exploit within the Crusader states. Raymond managed to secure his release only after paying a massive ransom or through diplomatic negotiations. Upon his return he found the kingdom of Jerusalem in disarray due to succession crises and internal feuds. He aligned himself with different kings to maintain his own authority against rivals like Miles of Plancy.\n\n*Relationship with Baldwin IV and Legacy*\nRaymond served as regent for the young and ill King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. He acted as a guardian for the boy king while also protecting the realm from Saladin. The relationship between them was often strained due to Raymond own ambitions and the pressure from other nobles. He later supported Guy of Lusignan which caused further division among the Christian leaders. Raymond died in 1187 just before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin that led to the fall of Jerusalem. His death removed a stabilizing force from the Latin East at a critical moment. The legacy of Raymond III remains significant because he navigated the dangerous political landscape of the Crusader states with skill. His actions influenced the course of history in the Holy Land during the twelfth century. He is remembered as a shrewd politician who prioritized the survival of his county above all else.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0023262715}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.05213126}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.008582}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.8389683}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.011938444}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -1.2692287}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.076266326}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.028271992}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00058680103}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0098882625}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.06642527}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.012784677}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -7.867844e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -5.7220623e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.05830753}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.29482424}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -1.0018066}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.27929133}, {"token": " position", "logprob": -1.0044056}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.41665766}, {"token": " many", "logprob": -0.09119726}, {"token": " years", "logprob": -0.29457086}, {"token": " while", "logprob": -1.5246904}, {"token": " serving", "logprob": -0.7780135}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.007334}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.1849719}, {"token": " key", "logprob": -1.387306}, {